Subject: [PATCH] vm: flush the faulting page after fault-around

Fault-around may populate a nearby page or find it already mapped. CPUs
that cache invalid page-table entries can otherwise repeatedly fault on
that page even though its permissions allow the access. Flush the faulting
page on the current CPU, as the single-page fault handler already does.
Also flush after a neighboring page fails: the faulting page may already
be mapped, and the caller can then return success despite that error.

---
diff --git a/kernel/core/src/vm/vmar/vm_mapping.rs b/kernel/core/src/vm/vmar/vm_mapping.rs
--- a/kernel/core/src/vm/vmar/vm_mapping.rs
+++ b/kernel/core/src/vm/vmar/vm_mapping.rs
@@ -393,6 +393,10 @@ impl VmMapping {
                 rss_delta,
             );
 
+            // Fault-around may have installed mappings even if a neighboring page failed.
+            TlbFlushOp::for_range(page_aligned_addr..page_aligned_addr + PAGE_SIZE)
+                .perform_on_current();
+
             // Errors caused by the "around" pages should be ignored, so here we
             // only return the error if the faulting page is still not mapped.
             if res.is_err() {
